Diagnosis, established from the bytecode of the failing method rather than inferred:

  $ javap -p -c -cp io.joern.flatgraph-core_3-0.1.32.jar flatgraph.storage.WriterContext
    private static final java.nio.IntBuffer finish$$anonfun$2(
        java.io.ByteArrayOutputStream, java.nio.IntBuffer, java.lang.String);
       4: invokevirtual  // java/lang/String.getBytes:(Ljava/nio/charset/Charset;)[B
      10: invokevirtual  // java/io/ByteArrayOutputStream.write:([B)V
      16: invokevirtual  // java/nio/IntBuffer.put:(I)Ljava/nio/IntBuffer;

  and the field the enclosing finish() iterates:
    private final scala.collection.mutable.LinkedHashMap stringpool;

  So flatgraph serializes the graph's ENTIRE deduplicated string pool by UTF-8 encoding each
  string and appending it to ONE java.io.ByteArrayOutputStream, recording lengths in a parallel
  IntBuffer. A ByteArrayOutputStream is backed by a single contiguous byte[], and the JVM caps
  an array at Integer.MAX_VALUE - 8 = 2,147,483,639 elements. The reported requirement was
  '2147483639 + 72', i.e. the buffer was already at that cap and the next string did not fit.

  Consequences that follow directly, and matter for what can and cannot be done about it:
    - The bound is on ONE array's length, not on the heap. Raising -Xmx cannot move it; the
      failure would occur identically at any heap size.
    - The bound is a function of the total UTF-8 size of the graph's distinct strings — method
      full names, type full names, file names, literals — so it scales with the breadth of the
      input set, not with the time or memory spent building the graph.
    - The graph was fully BUILT in memory. It is the persistence step that cannot represent it.
      Nothing about the in-memory graph's correctness is in question; it simply cannot be written.

Occurrences of the failure signature in the frontend's output, so the count is not left implied:
  1  lines matching 'Required array length'
  2  lines matching 'OutOfMemory' — of which one is the failure above and the remainder are
     'Overwriting class file: .../SparkOutOfMemoryError.class' warnings, i.e. a Spark class name

