STEP 9 — The partial output — recorded as evidence, explicitly NOT accepted
====================================================================================================
The writer had already flushed part of the graph before it failed, leaving a file at the output
path. It is a truncated write from a terminated serialization and is not a graph.

  path        /tmp/blitzy/scratch/f38258d3-f87d-44f5-bedc-af512c69e0ab/w-005/cpg/spark.cpg.PARTIAL-TRUNCATED-DO-NOT-LOAD
  bytes       691,541,019
  sha256      b1559c930a7b9ced717a0babf9a7e172d2b93d2cdef45a959304f063aedfe408
  accepted    False

It has been renamed to spark.cpg.PARTIAL-TRUNCATED-DO-NOT-LOAD in the run's private scratch, it
was never linked at harness/cpg/spark.cpg, and no stage loaded it. The plan's rule that a
truncated graph's silence is indistinguishable from a clean result is precisely why: accepting
this file would have produced per-module verdicts that looked like findings about Spark and were
in fact findings about a broken write.

====================================================================================================
STEP 10 — Mitigations examined, and why each is unavailable
====================================================================================================
Each was checked against the frontend's actual flag surface and against the plan's constraints,
not dismissed on judgement.

  The frontend's complete option set (jimple2cpg --help), verbatim:
    Usage: jimple2cpg [options] input-dir
    
      input-dir                source directory
      -o, --output <value>     output filename
      --exclude <file1>        files or folders to exclude during CPG generation (paths relative to <input-dir> or absolute paths)
      --exclude-regex <value>  a regex specifying files to exclude during CPG generation (paths relative to <input-dir> are matched)
      --enable-early-schema-checking
                               enables early schema validation during AST creation (disabled by default)
      --enable-file-content    add the raw source code to the content field of FILE nodes to allow for method source retrieval via offset fields (disabled by default)
      --help                   display this help message
      --android <value>        Optional path to android.jar while processing apk file.
      --full-resolver          enables full transitive resolution of all references found in all classes that are resolved
      --recurse                recursively unpack jars
      --depth <value>          maximum depth to recursively unpack jars, default value 1
      --dynamic-dirs <dir1>,<dir2>,...
                               Mark all class files in dirs as classes that may be loaded dynamically. Comma separated values for multiple directories.
      --dynamic-pkgs <pkg1>,<pkg2>,...
                               Marks all class files belonging to the package pkg or any of its subpackages as classes which the application may load dynamically. Comma separated values for multiple packages.

  1. Raise the heap. Examined and rejected on the evidence in STEP 8: the bound is a fixed array
     length, independent of heap. 128 GiB was already committed and peak RSS was 113 GiB, so the
     process had headroom when it failed.
  2. --exclude / --exclude-regex, or dropping pre-shade, -tests or shims artifacts. This is the
     one lever that would work, and it is prohibited: the plan requires the graph built over every
     JAR the build produced, excluding nothing — 'not the connect-shims artifact, not a -tests
     artifact, not a pre-shade sibling' — and lists trimming the input set among the conditions
     that stop the run rather than get repaired.
  3. Drop --recurse, or bound --depth. Same class as (2): both reduce what is read from the input
     set, and --recurse is mandated.
  4. A frontend or flatgraph version whose writer chunks the string pool. Prohibited: the plan
     forbids installing, upgrading or substituting any tool, and a graph built by a different
     frontend than the pinned one would not be the graph the plan specifies in any case.
  5. Split the input and merge the graphs. The frontend offers no merge, and no CPG-level merge
     exists in the pinned distribution; a merged graph would in any case have the same string
     pool and hit the same bound at write time.
  6. Run the frontend to build the graph and query it in-process without persisting. The plan
     requires the graph persisted and its identity recorded and re-verified before every later
     load, and the runners read it from disk by path. An unpersisted graph satisfies neither.

  So the input set the plan mandates and the writer the pinned frontend ships are not
  simultaneously satisfiable on any host. This is a property of the toolchain at this input
  breadth, reported rather than worked around.

====================================================================================================
STEP 11 — Provenance of overwritten entries — the stated limitation
====================================================================================================
The input set is reproducible byte for byte: the staging tree of invocation A survives on disk and
every one of its 191 members was re-hashed against the asserted manifest in STEP 1 above, 191 of
191 identical, 0 drifted. STEP 1 of this file is the owner of that assertion. Two files that could
be mistaken for its owner are not: cpg-input-inventory.json inventories the 62-archive provisioned
staging tree, and cpg-frontend-input-manifest.json carries the ordered per-entry manifest of
invocation C's 189-archive set (see the INVOCATION INDEX above). A persisted ordered per-entry
manifest of invocation A's 191 members is not among the files of this tree; what is persisted is
the assertion over them, re-measured in STEP 1. But the frontend's directory walk is not ordered by this run, and its
overwrite warning names the destination entry rather than the archive that supplied the surviving
definition. Per-entry provenance for overwritten entries is therefore NOT measurable from this
frontend's output, and no winner map is claimed. Where a specific collision matters to a
conclusion, it is resolved by querying the graph for that class and reporting what is there.

