PART 2 below records a second, narrower frontend invocation run to establish what is measurable
in spite of this: a per-module witness graph over one primary artifact per JAR-producing module.
It is labelled throughout as a capability measurement. It is NOT the graph the plan mandates, it
is NOT written to the sanctioned path, and no runner loads it.

####################################################################################################
PART 2 — THE PER-MODULE WITNESS GRAPH
####################################################################################################

WHAT THIS IS, AND WHAT IT IS NOT. PART 1 establishes that a graph over the complete input set
the plan mandates cannot be persisted by the pinned frontend. That leaves a question still
answerable and worth answering: for each JAR-producing module, does this frontend produce that
module's own classes into a graph at all? PART 2 answers exactly that and nothing more.

  - It is a FRONTEND-CAPABILITY MEASUREMENT over a deliberately narrower input set.
  - It is NOT the graph AAP 0.5.1 mandates, and it does not satisfy D1.
  - It is NOT written to harness/cpg/spark.cpg, and that path is unchanged by it.
  - NO runner loads it, and it contributes NO row to findings.json or findings.csv.
  - Its per-module results are reported in oss-scan-results/build-record.md section 6 as a
    SECOND, separately labelled column, never as a verdict against the graph any runner reads.

Why this input set, stated so the choice is checkable rather than convenient: exactly ONE
artifact per JAR-producing module — that module's PRIMARY artifact, its main unclassified,
non-original-, non--tests JAR. That is the minimal set in which every module's own witness can
be sought, and it is chosen by a rule rather than by which modules were missing.

